WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.321
This street is not camera footage.

00:00:02.321 --> 00:00:05.750
Tesla's new Reality View draws it, live, from above the car.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.604
People guessed Gaussian splatting.

00:00:07.604 --> 00:00:12.762
By Musk's account, it is a separate generator reading the driving model's own features.

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:16.149
I'm Matte.

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:19.063
Nine posts on seven subjects went past this week.

00:00:19.063 --> 00:00:23.272
I read what each one pointed at, starting with a paper about transparency.

00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:28.645
AlphaAnything gives image generators a fourth channel, opacity.

00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:33.688
It keeps the pretrained decoder frozen, stage by stage, latent to colour.

00:00:34.108 --> 00:00:36.284
Beside it runs a small alpha branch.

00:00:36.284 --> 00:00:38.772
At every stage it reads the colour features.

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:40.637
The arrows only point one way.

00:00:41.057 --> 00:00:42.625
Nothing from alpha goes back.

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:46.075
So for any latent, the colour comes out exactly as the

00:00:46.075 --> 00:00:47.330
original decoder makes it.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.024
But the alpha loss flows back through the frozen decoder into the LoRA.

00:00:52.024 --> 00:00:54.325
The latent itself learns to carry opacity.

00:00:56.242 --> 00:01:00.654
From text it makes separate transparent layers, which the authors arranged by hand.

00:01:01.034 --> 00:01:04.011
From a finished image it pulls each object out as a cutout.

00:01:05.092 --> 00:01:09.050
On extraction its masks overlap the truth far better than the baselines.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:12.018
Its colour on the FLUX version is worse, though.

00:01:13.658 --> 00:01:15.972
TypeGPU Kit is fifty WebGPU modules.

00:01:15.972 --> 00:01:19.058
Each one records into the frame you own.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.563
Here each line of the frame function lands in one stage: camera,

00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:24.938
shadow, scene, bloom, finish.

00:01:26.392 --> 00:01:29.008
The shaders are TypeScript, marked use gpu.

00:01:29.408 --> 00:01:32.404
At build time a plugin parses each one and keeps its

00:01:32.404 --> 00:01:33.493
syntax tree beside it.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:38.262
When a pipeline is made, TypeGPU walks that tree and writes WGSL.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.979
Pine is an agent harness with a task board.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:46.252
Here one slash command turns a request into seven tasks.

00:01:46.632 --> 00:01:47.716
Five sub-agents take them.

00:01:47.716 --> 00:01:50.154
Agents do not talk to each other on it.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.918
If tasks are still open when the lead stops, the daemon calls it back.

00:01:55.338 --> 00:01:58.299
When everything is done, a sub-agent that did none of the

00:01:58.299 --> 00:01:59.375
work checks the answer.

00:01:59.795 --> 00:02:01.875
No model decides the job is finished.

00:02:01.875 --> 00:02:02.767
The board does.

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:07.500
visloc-rs ports Basalt and COLMAP's mapper to Rust.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.701
Its README says it beats ORB-SLAM3 on 9 of 11 EuRoC sequences.

00:02:13.081 --> 00:02:15.148
It also lists where it still loses.

00:02:15.148 --> 00:02:17.805
That paragraph is the reason to trust the rest.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:23.221
On Vercel's UI SDK, ui-feed shows each component in a costume, with

00:02:23.221 --> 00:02:24.965
a prompt you can copy.

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:30.956
We fork off from these UVE features, then run a separate,

00:02:30.956 --> 00:02:32.707
roughly one-billion-parameter diffusion model.

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:36.871
And a hobbyist's version of a room, from four webcams.

00:02:36.871 --> 00:02:39.992
It smears where the cameras cannot see, and he has not

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:41.127
said how it works.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.851
The idea I would keep: a new output can read a frozen

00:02:45.851 --> 00:02:49.644
decoder and leave its colour, for a given latent, exactly as it was.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.834
A one-way alpha branch, modules that borrow your frame, and a board

00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:56.837
that decides when the work is done.

00:02:57.257 --> 00:02:59.253
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:59.253 --> 00:02:59.823
I'm Matte.

00:02:59.823 --> 00:03:00.108
Bye!
